trivial|[trivial] Add a unit-test harness to contracts/mock_token|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/mock_token/Cargo.toml`|The mock SEP-41 token backs every registry test that does not use the real SAC, but it has no tests of its own, so a regression in transfer or allowance handling would only appear indirectly.|Add a `#[cfg(test)] mod test` with a shared setup helper that initialises the token and registers a client;Cover `init` plus one happy-path transfer through the generated client;Assert the token's views are callable through `token::Client`|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs` shows the existing harness pattern;`soroban_sdk::testutils::Address as _` for generating actors
trivial|[trivial] Test that mock_token rejects a second init|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`init` is the only way to appoint the minter, so its one-shot guard is security relevant and currently untested.|Call `init` twice and assert `TokenError::AlreadyInitialized`;Assert the second call does not replace the stored admin;Assert `admin()` still returns the first account|The registry has an equivalent test at `test_double_init_is_rejected`
trivial|[trivial] Cover mock_token mint authorization and amount validation|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`mint` is the supply-changing path and must remain admin-only with positive amounts.|Assert a non-admin mint fails with `TokenError::Unauthorized`;Assert zero and negative mint amounts fail with `TokenError::InvalidAmount`;Assert repeated mints accumulate in the recipient balance|The admin lookup and auth order are in `MockToken::mint`
trivial|[trivial] Test mock_token transfer balances and insufficient funds|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`move_balance` is shared by `transfer` and `transfer_from`, so its debit and credit rules protect every mock-token flow used by the suite.|Assert a transfer moves the exact amount between balances;Assert a transfer above the source balance fails with `TokenError::InsufficientBalance`;Assert neither balance changes when the transfer fails|`read_balance` and `move_balance` are the code under test
trivial|[trivial] Test that mock_token transfer authenticates the sender|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|A token that lets anyone spend another account's balance would invalidate every escrow test that relies on it.|Invoke `transfer` without authorizing `from` and assert the host rejects it;Assert the same call succeeds when `from` authorizes it;Assert a third-party authorization does not authorise the debit|Use `env.mock_auths` with an explicit auth list rather than `mock_all_auths`
trivial|[trivial] Cover mock_token approve and allowance overwrite|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`approve` stores an allowance and an expiry, and its overwrite behaviour is assumed by `transfer_from`.|Assert a negative approval fails with `TokenError::InvalidAmount`;Assert `allowance` returns the approved amount;Assert a second approval overwrites rather than adds to the first|`DataKey::Allowance` and `DataKey::AllowanceExpiry` are written by `approve`
trivial|[trivial] Test mock_token allowance expiry at the ledger boundary|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`read_allowance` compares `live_until_ledger` against the current ledger sequence, and off-by-one behaviour would silently grant or deny a spender.|Assert a live allowance is readable at exactly `live_until_ledger`;Assert it returns `0` once the sequence passes that ledger;Assert `live_until_ledger = 0` means the allowance never expires|`env.ledger().set_sequence_number` is the test knob
trivial|[trivial] Test mock_token transfer_from decrements allowance|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`transfer_from` both spends an allowance and moves balance, and a bug in either half would break integration tests that use allowances.|Assert an approved spender can transfer within the allowance;Assert the stored allowance decreases by exactly the transferred amount;Assert spending above the allowance fails with `TokenError::InsufficientAllowance` and moves nothing|`transfer_from` writes the allowance before calling `move_balance`
trivial|[trivial] Cover mock_token metadata and admin-before-init views|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|The token exposes default metadata and a `NotInitialized` admin error that clients depend on before deployment is complete.|Assert `decimals()` is `7`, `name()` is `KeyLease Mock Token` and `symbol()` is `KLMOCK` before init;Assert `admin()` fails with `TokenError::NotInitialized` before init;Assert the configured values survive a fresh read after init|`init` writes all three metadata keys
trivial|[trivial] Assert mock_token extends TTL on both balance entries|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|Balance entries are persistent and `move_balance` extends both sides after a transfer, but no test pins that storage behaviour.|Read the TTL of the source and destination balance keys after a transfer;Assert both are extended above `TTL_THRESHOLD`;Assert a mint also extends the recipient entry|`env.storage().persistent().get_ttl` is available in the test environment
trivial|[trivial] Pin the mock_token error discriminants|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|Mock-token codes are asserted in registry tests and in client integrations, so renumbering them would be an accidental ABI change.|Assert `InvalidAmount` is `1` through `Unauthorized` is `6`;Add a test that fails if a new variant is inserted before an existing one;Document in the test why the numbers are frozen|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs` has a similar stability test for `Error`
trivial|[trivial] Test uninitialized registry views|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`get_admin` and `get_token` are the first calls a client makes after a deploy, and their pre-init errors are not covered.|Assert `get_admin` fails with `Error::NotInitialized` on a fresh registry;Assert `get_token` fails with `Error::TokenNotSet` on a fresh registry;Assert both succeed once `init` and `set_token` have run|`storage::get_admin` and `storage::get_token` return `Option`
trivial|[trivial] Test unknown service and lease lookups|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|The read views must distinguish a missing id from an internal failure, and callers branch on these exact errors.|Assert `get_service(1)` fails with `Error::ServiceNotFound` on an empty registry;Assert `get_lease(1)` fails with `Error::LeaseNotFound`;Assert `lease_status`, `remaining_calls` and `locked_balance` propagate the same errors|`storage::get_service` and `storage::get_lease` are the error sources
trivial|[trivial] Test the exact lease expiry boundary|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Expiry uses `now >= expires_at` in some paths and `now < expires_at` in others, so the boundary needs a dedicated test.|Advance the ledger to exactly `expires_at` and assert `lease_status` is `Expired`;Assert `remaining_calls` is `0` at that timestamp;Assert a partial settlement is allowed at the boundary|Existing tests cover before and after but not the exact equality
trivial|[trivial] Test the exact revoke grace boundary|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|The consumer may revoke at `expires_at + SETTLE_GRACE_PERIOD_SECS`, and an off-by-one would lock funds for an extra second or release them early.|Advance to exactly `expires_at + grace` and assert revocation succeeds;Assert one second earlier fails with `Error::RevokeWindowNotElapsed`;Assert the refund equals the full `locked_deposit`|`test_revoke_during_grace_window_is_rejected` covers the inside of the window
trivial|[trivial] Test a lease with u32::MAX allocated calls|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`allocated_calls` is a `u32` and the deposit calculation casts it to `i128`, which is the largest user-controlled multiplier in the protocol.|Register a service with rate `1` and create a lease for `u32::MAX` calls;Assert the stored `allocated_calls` and `locked_deposit` are exact;Assert settlement for the full allotment pays exactly the deposit and refunds zero|`create_lease` casts with `requested_calls as i128`
trivial|[trivial] Test independent leases on the same service|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Settlement and revocation mutate per-lease state, and a shared-service bug could let one lease release another's escrow.|Create two leases for one service and settle them with different call counts;Assert each payout and refund depend only on its own lease;Assert the contract balance after both settlements is zero|`Lease` state is keyed by `DataKey::Lease(lease_id)`
trivial|[trivial] Test that a paused service can still settle existing leases|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Deactivation is documented to block only new leases, but the settlement path reads the service without checking `is_active`.|Create a lease then deactivate its service;Assert `create_lease` now fails with `Error::ServiceInactive`;Assert `settle_lease` on the existing lease still succeeds with the correct payout|`set_service_active` only changes the stored flag
trivial|[trivial] Test that reactivating a service accepts new leases|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|A provider that pauses a service must be able to resume it, and the flag has two transitions that deserve coverage.|Deactivate then reactivate a service;Assert `is_active` is true in `get_service`;Assert a new lease can be created and settled after reactivation|Pair this with the paused-service test above
trivial|[trivial] Test that setting the same service status is idempotent|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`set_service_active` can be called with the current value, and clients may retry it after a timeout.|Call it twice with `false` and assert the second call succeeds;Assert the stored service is still inactive;Assert one `service_status_changed` event is emitted per call|Decide and document whether the repeat event is intended
trivial|[trivial] Assert the exact lease_created event topics and payload|`contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|Indexers filter by `lease_created` plus `lease_id`, so the topic order and every payload field are part of the public interface.|Assert the first topic is `lease_created` and the second is the new `lease_id`;Assert all six payload fields match the created lease;Assert no extra fields are present|`assert_single_event` is used by the existing event tests
trivial|[trivial] Assert event ordering across a full lease lifecycle|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|A create-then-settle or create-then-revoke flow should emit exactly the documented transitions, and extra or missing events break accounting pipelines.|Assert create plus settle emits `lease_created` then `lease_settled`;Assert create plus revoke emits `lease_created` then `lease_revoked`;Assert no third lease event is emitted by either flow|Use `env.events().all()` to inspect ordering
trivial|[trivial] Test the registry_initialized event|`contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|The first event a deployment emits proves which admin was appointed, but no test asserts its payload.|Call `init` and assert one `registry_initialized` event;Assert the payload admin equals the stored admin;Assert a rejected second `init` emits nothing|`RegistryInitialized` is defined in `events.rs`
trivial|[trivial] Test the contract_upgraded event|`contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|Upgrades are the highest-trust admin action, and the event is the only on-chain signal that the Wasm changed.|Call `upgrade` with a fixed 32-byte hash and assert one `contract_upgraded` event;Assert the payload contains the acting admin and the same hash;Assert a non-admin upgrade emits nothing|Follow the setup used by `test_upgrade_is_admin_only`
trivial|[trivial] Test that a failed create_lease leaves counters untouched|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`next_lease_id` runs before the token transfer, so a revert must roll the counter back or ids would be skipped after any failed payment.|Force the token transfer to fail and assert the call returns an error;Assert `lease_count` is unchanged;Assert the next successful lease reuses the expected id|The Soroban host rolls back storage on a failed invocation
trivial|[trivial] Test the smallest possible lease|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|The minimum valid inputs are one call, rate one and duration one second, and they exercise every checked-math path without masking errors in larger numbers.|Register rate `1` and create a lease with one call and one second;Assert `locked_deposit` is `1`;Assert settlement after expiry pays `1` and refunds `0`|This is the lower bound of the accepted input domain
trivial|[trivial] Test settling zero calls after expiry|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|A provider that could not serve a lease should be able to return the entire deposit through settlement, not only through consumer revocation.|Create a lease, advance past expiry and settle with `verified_calls = 0`;Assert `payout` is `0` and `refund` equals the deposit;Assert the lease is `Settled` and the consumer balance is restored|`settle_lease` skips zero-value transfers
trivial|[trivial] Assert that revocation reports the Settled status|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|There is no separate Revoked variant, so clients need a test proving how a revoked lease appears to the views.|Revoke an expired lease and assert `get_lease().settled` is true;Assert `lease_status` returns `LeaseStatus::Settled`;Assert `remaining_calls` and `locked_balance` return zero|`LeaseStatus` is derived in `types.rs`
trivial|[trivial] Test a lease created at ledger timestamp zero|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|The test network can start at timestamp zero, and expiry arithmetic must not treat zero as a missing value.|Set the ledger timestamp to `0`, create a lease and assert `expires_at == duration_secs`;Assert the lease is `Active` at timestamp zero;Assert a partial settlement is rejected until expiry|`env.ledger().set_timestamp(0)` is the setup
trivial|[trivial] Test that a rejected service registration does not consume an id|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`register_service` validates the rate before calling `next_service_id`, and that order is what keeps ids contiguous.|Call `register_service` with rate `0` and assert `Error::InvalidRate`;Assert `service_count` is unchanged;Assert the next valid registration receives id `1`|Validation order inside `register_service`
trivial|[trivial] Test a deposit that consumes the consumer's entire balance|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|The escrow transfer must succeed when the deposit equals the balance exactly, with no implicit fee or rounding.|Fund the consumer with exactly the required deposit;Create the lease and assert the consumer balance is zero;Settle and assert the consumer receives the expected refund and no more|Use the mock token or the real SAC for the funding step
trivial|[trivial] Test that fresh counters start at zero|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`service_count` and `lease_count` are documented as monotonic totals, and their initial values are part of the read interface.|Assert both views return `0` before any mutation;Assert `lease_count` increments only on a successful create;Assert `service_count` increments only on a successful registration|Counters live in instance storage
trivial|[trivial] Pin the registry error discriminants|`contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|Error numbers are ABI and are compared by clients and by `try_*` tests, so a reordered enum is a breaking change.|Assert variants `1` through `16` map to their documented names;Add a test that fails when a new variant is inserted in the middle;Document that new codes must be appended|The table in `docs/contract-reference.md` is the reference
trivial|[trivial] Test that a missing token leaves no draft lease|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`create_lease` resolves the token before allocating a lease id, and a failure at that point must not leave readable state.|Call `create_lease` before `set_token` and assert `Error::TokenNotSet`;Assert `lease_count` is unchanged;Assert `get_lease` still fails with `LeaseNotFound`|The token lookup happens before `next_lease_id`
trivial|[trivial] Test conservation of value across a mixed batch|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Single-lease tests do not catch accounting drift between leases, especially when one is settled and another is revoked.|Create three leases with different calls and rates;Settle one, revoke one after grace and leave one active;Assert payouts plus refunds equal all deposits released and the contract balance equals the remaining escrow|Balance the mock token or SAC accounts explicitly in the test
trivial|[trivial] Add a glossary to the documentation|`docs/index.md`, `mkdocs.yml`|Terms such as lease, service, consumer, grace window and stroop are used across the docs but never defined in one place.|Create a glossary page covering every protocol term used in the guides;Link each term to the page that explains it in depth;Add the page to `mkdocs.yml` and `docs/SUMMARY.md`|`docs/introduction.md` and `docs/protocol.md` are the sources
trivial|[trivial] Document rate units and stroop conversion|`docs/contract-reference.md`, `docs/consumer-guide.md`|`rate_per_call` is an `i128` in the token's smallest unit, and readers can easily mistake it for whole tokens or for XLM.|State the unit for the native SAC and for custom SEP-41 tokens;Show a worked conversion for the demo rate `1000000`;Warn that a token's decimals change the human-readable price|`docs/deployments.md` quotes the demo rate in stroops
trivial|[trivial] Document the exact grace-window boundaries|`docs/protocol.md`|The settlement window is described in prose but the inclusive and exclusive comparisons are not stated, and integrators schedule against them.|Add a table for `now < expires_at`, `now == expires_at`, `now < expiry + grace` and the first revocable second;State which entry points are allowed in each row;Cross-link the boundary tests|`SETTLE_GRACE_PERIOD_SECS` is defined in `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`
trivial|[trivial] Add a protocol FAQ|`docs/faq.md`, `mkdocs.yml`, `docs/SUMMARY.md`|Recurring questions about refunds, missed settlements, testnet resets and key management are scattered across guides.|Answer at least the refund, grace-window, key-loss, token-choice and testnet-reset questions;Link each answer to the detailed page;Add the page to both navigation files|`docs/security.md` and `docs/networks.md` answer most of these today
trivial|[trivial] Add a troubleshooting page|`docs/troubleshooting.md`, `mkdocs.yml`|Users hit `LeaseNotSettleable`, `RevokeWindowNotElapsed` and missing-service errors and have to infer the cause from the error table.|Group the common failures by actor and show the fix for each;Include RPC and testnet-reset symptoms with commands to verify state;Link every error code to the contract reference|The error table is in `docs/contract-reference.md`
trivial|[trivial] Document running the registry with a multisig admin|`docs/security.md`, `docs/deployment.md`|The docs recommend a cold or multisig admin but never explain how Stellar account auth makes that work with `require_auth`.|Explain that the admin can be a Stellar multisig account and that the host enforces its signing rules;Show the deploy and `upgrade` flow with a multisig signer;State what the contract can and cannot enforce about the signer set|`Address::require_auth` delegates to the account's own auth rules
trivial|[trivial] Compare the native SAC with a custom SEP-41 token|`docs/networks.md`|The registry accepts either token, and operators need the trust and operational trade-offs in one place.|Compare issuance, trustlines, decimals and admin controls for the native SAC and a custom token;State how the escrow guarantees differ, if at all;Recommend one for testnet and one for a future mainnet deployment|`docs/deployments.md` uses the native SAC on Testnet
trivial|[trivial] Add event-indexing recipes for every event|`docs/integration.md`|The event table names each event but only sketches what to do with it, leaving indexers to invent filters and bookkeeping.|Give a filter and an action for `lease_created`, `lease_settled`, `lease_revoked`, `service_status_changed` and `contract_upgraded`;Show how ids appear as topics for each event;Note which fields are payload-only|`docs/integration.md` already has the event table
trivial|[trivial] Explain LeaseStatus versus the settled flag|`docs/contract-reference.md`, `docs/protocol.md`|`lease_status` collapses revocation and settlement into `Settled`, which surprises clients that try to distinguish the two outcomes.|Document that `settled` is true after either settlement or revocation;Show how to read `used_calls` and the events to tell the paths apart;Add an example table of status by lifecycle event|`LeaseStatus::of` in `contracts/registry/src/types.rs`
trivial|[trivial] Document the release process|`docs/developer-guide.md`, `docs/releases/`|Releases exist, but a contributor cannot tell how the version, changelog, tag and docs are produced.|Describe the version bump, release-notes file, tag and checks required before tagging;List the artifacts and hashes that must be recorded;Link the existing release notes as examples|`Cargo.toml` carries the workspace version
trivial|[trivial] Add an upgrade safety checklist to the deployment guide|`docs/deployment.md`|The upgrade section shows commands but not the storage-compatibility checks an operator must run before installing a new Wasm.|List the invariants an upgrade must preserve, including `DataKey` variants and stored value shapes;Add steps to compare `version()` and replay events after the upgrade;Reference the contract's storage layout table|`docs/contract-reference.md` documents the storage layout
trivial|[trivial] Add a Testnet reset runbook|`docs/networks.md`, `docs/deployments.md`|Testnet resets wipe contract ids and lease state, and the docs warn about it without giving an operator sequence.|Give the post-reset redeploy, re-init, token-set and re-register steps;Explain how to update `docs/deployments.md` and `.env`;Add a check for the old contract id failing after the reset|The testnet reset note is in `docs/networks.md`
trivial|[trivial] Document a local sandbox walkthrough|`docs/developer-guide.md`|Contributors can run unit tests but have no end-to-end example against a local Stellar network with the built Wasm.|Show starting a local network, funding identities and deploying the registry;Walk through creating and settling one lease;State which commands differ from Testnet|`scripts/deploy-testnet.sh` is the Testnet reference
trivial|[trivial] Add TTL semantics to the storage-layout table|`docs/contract-reference.md`|The storage table lists the keys but not which are instance or persistent, nor the extension policy that protects escrow.|Add a storage class and TTL policy column for every `DataKey`;Document the lease-specific extension in `storage::lease_ttl_extend`;Explain what happens when an entry is not extended|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs` is the source
trivial|[trivial] Document the protocol constants in one table|`docs/contract-reference.md`|`MAX_LEASE_DURATION_SECS`, `SETTLE_GRACE_PERIOD_SECS` and the ledger TTL constants are scattered across code and prose.|List each constant with its value, unit and why it exists;State which constants bound user input and which only configure storage;Cross-link the relevant entry points|Constants are in `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs` and `storage.rs`
